Speaking of not knowing the policies...

The NSF policy (available for FTP from "ftp.eff.org" in
"pub/academic/policies/nsf") specifies acceptable and unacceptable
uses of the NSF backbone (only).  Being "from a commercial service"
has nothing to do with your need to worry or not worry about it.  In
theory, if your traffic crosses the NSF backbone, you have to worry
about the policy.  In truth, no one gives a rat's ass unless you're
doing something blatantly commercial.  I somehow doubt that posting to
"irrelated" (maybe "unrelated" or even "misrelated" would be a better
choice) newsgroups would put the screws on anyone.

The policy is couched in terms that allow its interpretation as
accepting all reasonable uses of the Internet (other than for-profit
activites) like so:

	SPECIFICALLY ACCEPTABLE USES:
		.
		.
		.
	(9) Communication incidental to otherwise acceptable use,
	    except for illegal or specifically unacceptable use.

or as disallowing almost all modern usage of the Internet:

	UNACCEPTABLE USES:
		.
		.
		.
	(11) Extensive use for private or personal business.

if one interprets "business" in a sense more general than commecial
enterprise.

Since it's so rarely clear whether or not your traffic has (at one
time or another) gone over the NSF backbone, and since the Internet
has expanded so far beyond the original backbone (both physically and
metaphysically, if you will) as to render the policy more than a
little archaic, said policy has---it would seem---fallen into disuse.

It would appear that this "threat" is rather empty, as might be
Mr./Ms. Mayhem's brainpan.  But that's mere speculation...

